# Line endings, and why this file had to exist before a second person cloned.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is true on the original machine and there was no
# `.gitattributes`, which means the byte content of every checkout was decided by
# the cloner's local git config rather than by the repository. CLAUDE.md rule 8
# spends a paragraph on the consequence: `tools/build.ps1` gates the export on
# `--verify`, so a check that pins the sha of raw file bytes is green in one
# working copy and red in every clone, worktree and CI run.
#
# MEASURED before this file landed, with `git ls-files --eol`: all 366 tracked
# text files were already `i/lf` in the index, but the working copy had split
# into 186 LF and 180 CRLF -- including the .gd sources themselves, 46 CRLF
# against 14 LF -- plus docs/index.html with mixed endings inside one file. That
# was ONE machine disagreeing with itself. A second machine makes it a coin flip.
#
# Because the index is already LF throughout, this file is index-neutral: adding
# it produces no content diff. It only makes checkout deterministic, and it makes
# the Windows dev boxes agree with the Linux runner in .github/workflows/verify.yml.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than a bare `text=auto` is deliberate. `text=auto` alone would
# hand each working copy its platform's native ending and leave a byte pin still
# unstable across the Windows/Linux boundary that CI has now introduced. Nothing
# here needs CRLF: `tools/gen/extract.js:436` already folds \r\n to \n, PowerShell
# 7 reads LF scripts, and Godot is indifferent.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicitly binary. These are already detected as binary by content, so this
# block changes nothing today -- it is here so that a future .png or .pck cannot
# be caught by the `text=auto` rule above and silently corrupted by an EOL pass.
# The web export in docs/ is the one that would hurt: a mangled .wasm or .pck
# ships a broken game to Pages and the assertion suite never sees it.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.webp  binary
*.wasm  binary
*.pck   binary
*.zip   binary
*.exe   binary
*.dll   binary
*.so    binary
*.dylib binary
*.ogg   binary
*.wav   binary
*.mp3   binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.pyc   binary

# The frame gate's committed reference images. Marked binary above by extension;
# called out here because `notes/perf/frames/ref/*.png` is the human half of the
# visual gate and a diff of it is meaningless -- the machine half is the numbers
# in golden.json, which IS a text diff and must stay one.
notes/perf/frames/ref/*.png binary

# Godot's generated sidecars. Text, and they must stay text: .import files carry
# the import parameters the project depends on, and a merge conflict in one needs
# to be readable rather than resolved by picking a side blindly.
*.import  text eol=lf
*.uid     text eol=lf
*.tscn    text eol=lf
*.gd      text eol=lf
*.godot   text eol=lf
